WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.420
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:12.120
In square ABCD, connect the corner A to the opposite side DC, dividing it into lengths of 1 &amp; 2.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.320
Repeat this procedure for the other 3 corners of the square.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.820
The result will be 4 lines that bound a square shown in blue.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.680
The question is what is the area of the square in blue,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.060
divided by the area of the square ABCD?

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.960
In other words, what fraction of the square ABCD is shaded in blue?

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.660
You can also solve the general problem where we replace 2 with the variable x.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.580
Pause the video if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:01:00.140
[Music]

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:06.020
One way to solve this problem is by considering the area of the parallelogram shown in green.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.080
We'll calculate its area in two different ways.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.230
One way to calculate the area is by taking the base of x and the height of x + 1.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:20.739
The area is then the product of the base and the height, which is equal to x multiplied by the quantity x + 1.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.370
But there's another way that we can calculate the area of the parallelogram.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:31.600
We'll take the height to be the length s of this square and we'll take the base to be the hypotenuse of this right triangle.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.740
We can calculate the length of this hypotenuse using the Gougu theorem.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.460
It'll be equal to the square root of 1 plus the quantity x + 1 squared.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.340
We then take the product of the base and the height to get another formula for the area of the parallelogram.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:50.499
We thus have two different ways to express the area of the parallelogram, so we set those equal to each other.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.680
We'll then solve this equation for s.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.569
We want the area of this square, which will be equal to s squared.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.009
We then want to divide it by the area of the entire square ABCD.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:07.540
This is equal to the quantity X + 1 squared.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:10.390
We thus take the ratio;

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:17.619
we'll then cancel out the x plus 1 squared terms and that will leave us with the fraction of the square that's shaded in blue.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.769
We can then solve for particular values.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:21.769
For example,

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:27.039
if x is equal to 2, we can easily calculate that the fraction that's shaded in blue

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:30.909
will be equal to 2 over 5, and that's the answer.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:34.239
It's a very neat geometry problem.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:42.519
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube. As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

